## The type argument

**Leading: Type 9, medium confidence. Strongest alternative: Type 4.**

The thread that survived: a man whose entire product is emotional disclosure and who cannot say a feeling to a person. Never been to therapy — *"Straight music, man."* The invisibility started as a legal constraint at 17 (too young to be inside the strip club, so he waited in the car, performed, left) and he says it became the method: *"that ended up being like my formula to stardom... you never really get to see me."*

**The discriminator against 4** is that he keeps spending real money and status to deny his own specialness: he rejects the word "pain" for his own music (*"it's not pain, it's not sad — it's normal, it's reality"*), refuses to correct a public image he says is wrong, and takes the blame himself for having no Grammys. Sharpest test — asked about heartbreak, he says he's never been the one left, because the moment she's ready to go *"that's going to bring me up to speed."* The Four's home territory is the one place he reports no experience.

## Two things you should know

**Risk flags are all on** (sensitive allegations, contested typing, source uncertainty), so this will require the six-role expanded jury rather than the standard two.

**The biggest weakness is honest and logged:** four of the most-quoted sources sit inside a nineteen-day album rollout, and every 2026 quote came from an unlabeled YouTube auto-transcript. The top research task is verifying each quotation against actual audio before any of it reaches a page — none should ship unchecked.
